1
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:54,064
<v CLERK> Good morning, ma’am, and welcome to “Australia’s Moving Experience”! How can I help you?

2
00:01:54,064 --> 00:01:59,703
<v WOMAN> Well, I... I hope you can help me. I’m so up in the air right now... I... 

3
00:01:59,703 --> 00:02:05,061
<v CLERK> Just calm down, now. Let me guess: you’re moving and it has you a little confused.

4
00:02:05,061 --> 00:02:12,976
<v WOMAN> That’s it exactly. You see, I’m relocating to the United States next month and I’m having a hard time getting organised.

5
00:02:12,976 --> 00:02:18,201
<v CLERK> Here, fill out your name and address, and let me ask you a few questions. Oh, what should I call you?

6
00:02:18,201 --> 00:02:20,522
<v WOMAN> My name is Jane, Jane Bond.

7
00:02:20,522 --> 00:02:26,815
<v CLERK> OK, Jane, first of all, what’s your work phone number? In case I have any questions about things.

8
00:02:26,825 --> 00:02:37,027
<v WOMAN> My work phone is 94635550. But please try not to call me too often there. My boss hates personal calls. 

9
00:02:37,037 --> 00:02:42,138
<v CLERK> So does mine, ma’am, so does mine. And what address should we ship your things to?

10
00:02:42,148 --> 00:02:56,133
<v WOMAN> My new company is letting me stay temporarily at 509 Clark House, that’s C-L-A-R-K, 1137 University Drive in Seattle. 

11
00:02:56,143 --> 00:03:06,014
<v CLERK> Seattle? Beautiful city, I hear. Mountains right beside the ocean, almost. Cooler than Australia, too. OK, and when should we come pack your things?

12
00:03:06,014 --> 00:03:09,271
<v WOMAN> I guess that would be on Monday, March 11th.

13
00:03:09,281 --> 00:03:15,075
<v CLERK> Do you want any help with an after-packing clean-up? We do that for a small additional charge.

14
00:03:15,085 --> 00:03:23,157
<v WOMAN> Yes, that would be helpful. I promised the landlord I’d give her the keys back by 5:00 p.m. on Thursday, the 14th. 

15
00:03:23,167 --> 00:03:29,452
<v CLERK> Great, we’ll just schedule the clean-up for that day. That way, the place will smell clean and there’ll be no dust.

16
00:03:29,462 --> 00:03:34,643
<v WOMAN> Well, you do think of everything! Oh, how much is this going to cost?

17
00:03:34,653 --> 00:03:36,708
<v CLERK> Here is a list of our basic prices.

18
00:03:36,718 --> 00:03:40,676
<v WOMAN> Oh dear, this seems rather expensive!

19
00:03:40,686 --> 00:03:48,845
<v CLERK> Yes ma’am, but you’re paying for the best. We’re careful and we’re fast. Like we say, the only thing we break are speed records getting you moved.

20
00:03:48,855 --> 00:04:06,878
<v WOMAN> Well... maybe that’s so... Oh, I nearly forgot to tell you. I don’t want my furniture shipped with me. I won't be looking for an apartment till after I arrive in America. Would it be possible to put my furniture in storage here for a month, then have it sent along later? 

21
00:04:06,888 --> 00:04:17,214
<v CLERK> Of course, we do that all the time. A couple of other things. Here at "A Moving Experience”, we try to pack your things logically. We don’t just throw stuff in boxes.

22
00:04:33,430 --> 00:04:40,203
<v CLERK> Do you have any special requests? You know, things you want packed in some special place, so you know where to find them?

23
00:04:40,213 --> 00:04:41,516
<v WOMAN> Like what? 

24
00:04:41,526 --> 00:04:47,003
<v CLERK> Oh, I don’t know... Things like dishes maybe. Not to be rude, but you look like a lady who likes to eat.

25
00:04:47,013 --> 00:04:52,074
<v WOMAN> Ahhh! Yes, I need my dishes and things where I can find them quickly.

26
00:04:52,084 --> 00:04:57,696
<v CLERK> Great. We'll put those dishes and cutlery in what we call the emergency pack. Can you think of anything else?

27
00:04:57,706 --> 00:05:08,911
<v WOMAN> Ummm, I do have an antique tea kettle my great-grandmother gave my mother. I wouldn’t want to lose that. So I guess you’d better put that in storage with the furniture.

28
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:16,804
<v CLERK> Grandma’s tea kettle with the furniture, got it! Say, how about things like your alarm clock? You don’t want to miss your plane on the big day, right?

29
00:05:16,804 --> 00:05:24,186
<v WOMAN> Well, you certainly think of everything! Yes, that’s right. I'll also need my alarm clock where I can find it.

30
00:05:24,196 --> 00:05:39,966
<v CLERK> Fine, we’ll put that in your personal package. And of course, we'll give you a list of where we pack everything. So, all you’ll have to do on Thursday, the 14th is grab your luggage on your way out the door. Um, I couldn’t help noticing the new CD player you’re carrying. Is that a Samsung? 

31
00:05:39,976 --> 00:05:45,642
<v WOMAN> Why? Yes, it is. One of their best. Cost me nearly a hundred dollars, it did!

32
00:05:45,652 --> 00:05:48,223
<v CLERK> Do you want to take special care of it? I mean it’s brand new. 

33
00:05:48,233 --> 00:05:54,162
<v WOMAN> Take care of it, but nothing special. You can just put it in storage with the furniture.

34
00:05:54,172 --> 00:05:57,245
<v CLERK> That looks like everything we need here. I guess you’re all set.

35
00:05:57,255 --> 00:06:03,850
<v WOMAN> That was certainly quick. Thank you, young man. This has been a most moving experience!